    fault: 'The lead elbow folding through impact (a chicken wing), often with a bent lead arm in the backswing too.',
    why: 'A lead arm that folds through the ball shortens the radius of the swing and leaves the clubface open, which tends to cause thin shots and slices. Swinging with the lead arm alone teaches it to stay long and rotate with the body.',
    kitAlt: 'Use the shortest, lightest club you have, held a few centimetres down the grip. At home, swing over a mat or rug and don\'t hit anything.',
    setup: 'Hold a short iron in your lead hand only, a little down the grip. Rest your trail hand on your lead shoulder (or put it behind your back).',
    steps: ['Hold a short iron in your lead hand only, a little down the grip, trail hand on your lead shoulder.',
      'Swing back slowly with your chest turning and the lead arm long.',
      'Swing through and keep the lead arm extended until it is well past where the ball would be.',
      'Let it fold only as it goes up into the finish.'],
    feel: ['A light grip with one hand.', 'The arm and chest turning together.', 'The arm long after the ball, pointing at the target.', 'Folding only at the end.'],
    sense: 'Your lead arm and chest turning together, and the arm still long and pointing down the target line just after the ball, before it folds up into the finish.',
    mistakes: [['Swinging too hard.', 'One arm can\'t control a fast swing. Keep it slow and smooth.'],
      ['Stopping the body and flicking with the arm.', 'Keep the chest turning so the arm can stay long.'],
      ['Gripping very tightly.', 'Hold just firmly enough to control the club.']],
    rx: '15 slow swings at about half speed, then 5 two-handed swings copying the same lead-arm feel. Rest your arm if it tires.',
    success: ['Just after impact, the lead arm is still straight and pointing down the target line.', 'The club brushes the ground in the same spot each time.', 'With both hands, finishes show a long lead arm through the ball.'],
