    fault: 'Rushing from the top: the arms and shoulders throw the club down first (casting), before the weight has moved to the lead side.',
    why: 'When the upper body starts the downswing, the club tends to move out and across the ball and release early, causing pulls, slices and weak contact. A full stop at the top removes the rush, so the next move has to come from the lower body.',
    kitAlt: 'At home, rehearse the pause and the first move without a ball.',
    setup: 'A 7-iron and a ball teed low. Make sure you have room to hold a full stop at the top.',
    steps: ['Swing to the top and complete your turn.',
      'Stop completely for about a second. Check you are balanced.',
      'Start down by moving your weight into your lead foot and letting your arms fall, not by pulling with the shoulders.',
      'Swing through to a full finish.',
      'Repeat, gradually shortening the pause until it is just a feeling of no rush.'],
    feel: ['A full turn.', 'Still and balanced at the top.', 'Lead foot first, arms falling.', 'Accelerating through the ball.', 'Smooth, unhurried transition.'],
    sense: 'A moment of stillness at the top, then pressure moving into your lead foot while your arms drop before your shoulders unwind.',
    mistakes: [['Pausing, then throwing the club from the top anyway.', 'The first move after the pause is the weight shift, not the arms.'],
      ['Swinging too hard after the pause.', 'Use three-quarter effort; the point is the order of moves.'],
      ['Losing balance during the pause.', 'If you can\'t hold the top, make a shorter backswing.']],
    rx: '10 balls with a full one-second pause, 10 with a shorter pause, then 5 normal swings keeping the same sequence.',
    success: ['You can hold the top for a second without wobbling.', 'You feel the lead foot take pressure before the arms swing down.', 'Ball flight starts more on line, with fewer pulls.'],
    progress: ['Pause at the top, no ball.', 'Balls with a one-second pause.', 'A shorter pause.', 'Normal swings with the same unhurried feel.'],
    note: 'Some players have a very smooth, almost paused transition and others a quick one. The drill is for golfers who rush; it doesn\'t mean everyone should be slow.',
